(require ycao-net/lib/markup)

(define post
  @article["Thoughts After Playing Death Stranding and Reflections on Art Criticism" "2025-11-15"]{
    @para{
      Today I finished Death Stranding. I must mention that while playing, I initially thought this was a post-pandemic work reflecting on pandemic isolation; I did not expect that the game actually released in 2019. What a coincidence.
    }
    @para{
      The most unique aspect of Death Stranding lies in its unification of authorial expression, narrative, and gameplay. Hideo Kojima's main creative intention is to convey the importance of mutual assistance between people; the game's story is precisely about Sam, as a “porter,” reconnecting a fragmented America in a post-apocalyptic world. The innovation is that the game's core gameplay—delivery—directly serves this theme. It constantly sets up a sharp contrast between “the hardship of delivering” and “the help brought by structures left by other players,” and the like system also gives players feedback when they help others. Most players have had similar emotional experiences (see @cite["ds-review-1"]).
    }
    @para{
      Reviews of Death Stranding have, unsurprisingly, been divided, mainly centering on its weak gameplay and monotonous play. @cite["ds-review-2"]@cite["ds-review-3"]Sylvester, in his Designing Games, proposes that games are “experience generation engines” @cite["sylvesterDesigningGamesGuide2013"], that is, they use the interactivity unique to games (mechanics, systems) to generate specific experiences (experience). The controversy over Death Stranding's gameplay stems from the fact that players did not expect its interactive (gameplay) portion to be deliberately designed as boring, difficult, and terrifying in order to set off its theme. But if analyzed from the perspective of experience transmission, players successfully felt the emotions—the “hardship,” “loneliness,” and “mutual assistance” that Hideo Kojima hoped players would experience—and this is a manifestation of successful game design. The traditional view holds that games must be “fun,” but fun is also an emotional experience, which can be subdivided concretely into a sense of achievement (common in sandboxes and competitive games), a sense of thrill (beautiful graphics, grand scenes, etc. (frequently used in popcorn movies too; it is a customary lazy trope)), and so on. Traditional game criticism expects that games can, and only can, bring “fun” through “interaction,” while ignoring other permutations and combinations—namely, that dynamic interaction (game mechanics) and traditional art (cutscenes, art, narrative) can express what traditional art expresses, such as the author's joys and sorrows and reflections on social issues.
    }
    @para{
      This evaluation framework can be further generalized: the difference between games and traditional entertainment lies in the fact that traditional entertainment products (literature, film, painting, etc.) use narrative and/or audiovisual means, whereas games use interactive means, but their goal is to convey emotional experience to the audience. To evaluate a work of literature, music, film, or game as “good” should mean that the author successfully made the reader experience the emotion he designed. When people exchange information, they need to prove certain propositions; the proof itself must be either rational (logos) or emotional (pathos). Persuading through reason makes an essay; moving through emotion makes art.
    }
    @para{
      Added on 2025/12/07: This article confuses “art evaluation” with “evaluation of an artist's creation”; the former is not well-defined/is still controversial.
    }
  })

(provide post)